__________________________________________________
 Purdue Honors Pledge
- “As a boilermaker pursuing academic excellence, I pledge to be honest and true in all that I do. Accountable together - we are Purdue.”
- “Academic integrity is one of the highest values that Purdue University holds. Individuals are encouraged to alert university officials to potential breaches of this value by either emailing integrity@purdue.edu or by calling 765-494-8778. While information may be submitted anonymously, the more information that is submitted provides the greatest opportunity for the university to investigate the concern.”


__________________________________________________
 Emergency Preparedness
- For emergency preparedness information, visit: [Purdue Emergency Preparedness](https://www.purdue.edu/ehps/emergency_preparedness/)


__________________________________________________
 Additional Help
- Purdue University is committed to advancing the mental health and well-being of its students. If you or someone you know is feeling overwhelmed, depressed, and/or in need of support, services are available. For help, contact Counseling and Psychological Services (CAPS) at (765)494-6995 and [Purdue CAPS](http://www.purdue.edu/caps/) during and after hours, on weekends and holidays, or visit the CAPS office on the second floor of the Purdue University Student Health Center (PUSH) during business hours.
- Purdue University strives to make learning experiences as accessible as possible. If you anticipate or experience physical or academic barriers based on disability, you are welcome to let me know so that we can discuss options. You are also encouraged to contact the Disability Resource Center at: drc@purdue.edu or by phone: 765-494-1247.


__________________________________________________
 Schedule
- **Lectures**: Notes, Code
- **Assignments**: Assignments
- **Syllabus**: 
  - Background: brief tutorials on linear algebra, geometry, coordinate systems, transformations, cameras, rendering, shaders, C# programming.
  - VR: head-mounted displays, tracking, redirected walking, 360 videos, haptics, collaboration, gaze tracking, foveated rendering, applications.
  - AR: optical & video see-through displays, annotation anchoring, diminished reality, apps.
  - VR and AR user studies: design, implementation, analysis, task load, and cybersickness.
  
The syllabus will be adapted according to time constraints and student interests.


__________________________________________________
 Required (but Fun) Work
- **Projects**: Four one-week projects worth 4 x 12% = 48%
- **Late Policy**: Once late (up to one week): no penalty; late second and subsequent times: 0 points for project; all projects are required by the last day of classes for completion of the course.
- **Regrading**: After you receive the grade for an assignment, you have exactly 1 week to ask for a regrade.
- **Final Project**: Worth 22% (includes project proposal, midterm presentation, final presentation).
- **Attendance**: Worth 5%.
- **Exams**: One midterm worth 25%; no final exam.


__________________________________________________
 Cheating Policy
- Do not cheat! Frequent and thorough scans for cheating will be conducted. If caught, automatic failing grade for the class and reported to the Dean of Students' Office.
- **Examples of Cheating** (courtesy of Gene Spafford):
  - Using part or all of someone else's work without prior approval.
  - Misrepresenting the functionality of code.
  - Using hidden notes during a test that does not allow open notes.
  - Submitting answers developed by another individual as your own.
  - Plagiarism: copying text without proper citation.
  - Setting permissions on files to allow copying by others.
  - Providing program code or solutions to another student without approval.
  - Encouraging or failing to report cheating activities.


