CS 34800: Information Systems Syllabus - Spring 2026

### Course Information
- **Course Title:** CS 34800: Information Systems
- **CRNs:** 13399 and 35118
- **Instructional Modality:** Face-to-Face
- **Course Credit Hours:** 3
- **Prerequisites:** Data Structures, Discrete Structures, and programming experience.

### Academic Excellence and Diversity

Purdue University encourages each individual to strive to reach their potential. The University is committed to academic excellence and seeks to develop and nurture diversity among its members. Diversity is believed to strengthen the institution, stimulate creativity, promote the exchange of ideas, and enrich campus life. For more information, refer to [Purdue’s nondiscrimination policy statement](#).

### Students With Disabilities

Purdue University strives to make learning experiences as accessible as possible. If you anticipate or experience physical or academic barriers due to a disability, you are encouraged to reach out to the Disability Resource Center at:

- Email: drc@purdue.edu
- Phone: 765-494-1247

Students are also welcome to inform the lead TA to discuss options. If you have a DRC recommendation letter and cannot participate in hotseat questions in class, please communicate this. For those with exam-time extensions, exams must be scheduled with the DRC testing center a few weeks before the exam date.

###

### Emergency Preparation

In the case of a significant campus emergency, course requirements, deadlines, and grading percentages may change due to a revised semester calendar or other uncontrollable circumstances. Relevant changes will be posted on Campuswire, the course schedule, and this syllabus. Instructors and TAs will also use direct emails to communicate with students. Students are expected to frequently check their Purdue email.

###

### Basic Need Support

If you are experiencing challenges in securing basic needs like food, housing, transportation, health services, or access to technology or childcare resources, and believe it may impact your course performance, please contact the Office of the Dean of Students (ODOS) for assistance in coordinating community resources. Services may vary by location. For West Lafayette, visit the Basic Needs Program website or email basicneeds@purdue.edu. If you are on the Indianapolis campus, you can connect with a Student Support Generalist by phone at 765-495-7797 or email studentlifeindy@purdue.edu.

###

### Mental Health Statement

If you are starting to feel stress, anxiety, or are overwhelmed, consider using WellTrack for support. Sign in to access a variety of tools and information. For additional support and resources, visit the Office of the Dean of Students during drop-in hours (M-F, 8 am-5 pm). Purdue University is dedicated to promoting the mental health and well-being of its students. If you or someone you know is feeling overwhelmed, depressed, or in need of mental health support, services are available. To seek help, contact Counseling and Psychological Services (CAPS) at 765-494-6995 at any time, including after hours, weekends, and holidays. You can also visit the CAPS office located on the second floor of the Purdue University Student Health Center (PUSH) during regular business hours.
================WEBPAGE: 3272=================

Purdue CS440: Large-scale Data Analytics (Spring 2026)

### Course Description
"Big data" has been a buzzword for a long time. Many disruptive techniques have been developed to address various aspects of big data. This course will cover the key concepts, design principles, and systems to analyze large-scale data in order to extract novel and transformative insights. 

**Tentative Topics Include:**
- Database fundamentals
- Big data storage (e.g., HDFS)
- Big data computing frameworks (e.g., Hadoop and Spark)
- Data warehouses
- Data lakes
- Graph analytics (e.g., Spark Graph)
- Data streaming (e.g., Spark Streaming)
- Large-scale machine learning (e.g., Spark MLlib)
- Vector databases
- Cloud-native data analytics

### Instructor
**Jianguo Wang**  
Email: [csjgwang@purdue.edu](mailto:csjgwang@purdue.edu) (note: must include "[CS440]" in the subject)

### Teaching Assistants
- Shige Liu: [liu3529@purdue.edu](mailto:liu3529@purdue.edu)
- Yunan Zhang: [zhan4404@purdue.edu](mailto:zhan4404@purdue.edu)
- Jiayi Liu: [liu4127@purdue.edu](mailto:liu4127@purdue.edu)

### Logistics
- **When:** MW 3:30p-4:20p
- **Where:** Wilmeth Active Learning Center 3087
- **Office Hour:** After class or by appointment
- **Pre-requisites:** CS242, CS251, and CS373

### Labs and PSOs
Labs and PSOs will start from the 3rd week.

**Lab Schedule:**
- L01: Tuesday 1:30pm-3:20pm (LWSN B131)
- L02: Friday 9:30am-11:20am (LWSN B131)
- L03: Wednesday 9:30am-11:20am (LWSN B131)
- L04: Wednesday 11:30am-1:20pm (LWSN B131)

### Online Communications
We will use **Piazza** for announcements, discussions, and Q&A. Brightspace will not be used except for occasional emails. We will use **Gradescope** for submitting and grading homeworks.

### Textbooks (Optional)
Note that textbooks are optional, and the lecture slides are self-contained.
- *Spark: The Definitive Guide* by Bill Chambers and Matei Zaharia.
- *Database System Concepts* (7th edition) by Avi Silberschatz, Henry F. Korth, and S. Sudarshan.
- *Mining of Massive Datasets* by Jure Leskovec, Anand Rajaraman, and Jeff Ullman.

### Grading
- Homeworks: 20% (2 * 10%)
- Midterm Exam: 25%
- Final Exam: 35%
- Project: 20% (2 * 10%)
- Projects are related to the labs and will be explained in the labs.
- Extra Credits: 5%

