final expanded context: CS 34800: Information Systems Syllabus - Spring 2026

### Course Information
- **Course Title:** CS 34800: Information Systems
- **CRNs:** 13399 and 35118
- **Instructional Modality:** Face-to-Face
- **Course Credit Hours:** 3
- **Prerequisites:** Data Structures, Discrete Structures, and programming experience.

### Course Description
This course introduces fundamental concepts of database management, primarily using the relational model. Key topics include:
- Schema design and refinement
- Query languages
- Database application development
- Transactions and concurrency control
- Overview of NoSQL databases (including graph and document stores)
- Overview of OLAP and data warehousing

### Course Comparisons
- **CS34800 vs. CS44800 vs. CS44000**
  - **CS34800:** Focuses on application development scenarios (designing and using a database system in web or mobile applications).
  - **CS44000:** Targets data-science and machine learning scenarios, emphasizing large-scale databases.
  - **CS44800:** Concentrates on database system implementation (database internals).

### Course Learning Outcomes
Students will learn to:
- Design effective database schemas that model real-world information semantics.
- Understand different data models (relational, graph, and document) utilized across database systems.
- Pose complex database queries using SQL, NoSQL languages, and theoretical query languages.
- Gain foundational knowledge in indexing.
- Understand transactions and concurrency control.
- Differentiate between OLTP and OLAP databases.

### Homework and TA Information
- **Lead TA:** Each homework will have a designated lead TA for special requests related to the homework (e.g., regrade requests, submission issues).
- **Project TA:** A project TA will be assigned to each student for project grading. Email your project TA with project-related questions.

#### Head TA Contact Information
- **Name:** Xi Pang
- **Email:** [pang65@purdue.edu](mailto:pang65@purdue.edu)

The head TA handles requests unrelated to specific homework or projects. Common issues include missed quizzes, extension requests, DRC-related accommodations, and makeup exams. Response time is within 24 hours on weekdays; follow up if no response is received.

### Instructor Information
- **Name:** Hisham Benotman
- **Email:** [hbenotma@purdue.edu](mailto:hbenotma@purdue.edu)
- **Office:** DSAI 1119G
- **Office Hours:** By appointment via email. Available after class for urgent questions.

### Teaching Assistants
- Xi Pang (Head TA) - [pang65@purdue.edu](mailto:pang65@purdue.edu)
- Yinqi Sun (HW1 Lead TA) - [sun1226@purdue.edu](mailto:sun1226@purdue.edu)
- Yeasir Rayhan (HW6 Lead TA) - [yrayhan@purdue.edu](mailto:yrayhan@purdue.edu)
- Chenzhe Jin (HW3 Lead TA) - [jin467@purdue.edu](mailto:jin467@purdue.edu)
- Xiao Hu - [hu953@purdue.edu](mailto:hu953@purdue.edu)
- Yunxin Sun (HW2 Lead TA) - [sun1114@purdue.edu](mailto:sun1114@purdue.edu)
- Yibo Wang (HW5 Lead TA) - [wang7342@purdue.edu](mailto:wang7342@purdue.edu)
- Xinzhi Wang (HW4 Lead TA) - [wang6171@purdue.edu](mailto:wang6171@purdue.edu)
- Jerry B. Liu - [jbliu@purdue.edu](mailto:jbliu@purdue.edu)

### TA Office Hours
TA office hours will take place every weekday from 4:00 PM to 6:00 PM (excluding holidays).  
Join TA office hours via this link: [Zoom link](https://purdue-edu.zoom.us/j/98460606670)  
In-person UTA office hours schedule will be posted soon.

### Textbook
- **Recommended:** Database System Concepts, 7th Edition by Avi Silberschatz, Henry F. Korth, and S. Sudarshan.
  - ISBN (Paperback): 978-1260084504
    - [Amazon Link](https://www.amazon.com/dp/126008450X)
  - ISBN (eTextbook, hardcover, or loose-leaf): 978-0078022159
    - [Amazon Link](https://www.amazon.com/dp/0078022158)

The textbook webpage provides additional resources such as slides, answers for practice questions, and an SQL interpreter at [db-book.com](http://www.db-book.com).

### Lectures
- **Meeting Times:** 
  - Indianapolis: **MW 5:30 PM to 6:45 PM**, ET 202.
  - West Lafayette: **TuTh 10:30 AM to 11:45 AM**, BHEE 129.

Lecture slides will be available at least one hour before the lecture. Recordings will be posted on Brightspace unless technical issues occur. They may be available as one or multiple videos. A subset of slides will be posted a few days after lectures. Note that solutions to in-class quizzes are not included in the posted materials. All class content is copyrighted.

### Course Schedule with Activity Due Dates
- **Schedule Information:** The schedule is tentative and subject to change. Due date changes will be highlighted in red.
  - View the schedule here: [Course Schedule](https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1mov5_UbuG9F6wt_QZWm8yGn4kfpkYqokqkzzgk0kwOU/edit?usp=sharing)

### Course Content and Links
- **Brightspace:** Content such as the syllabus, course schedule, lecture slides, and lecture videos will be uploaded to Brightspace. Quizzes and submission for some homework assignments will also be conducted via Brightspace.
- **Ed:** Class announcements and student questions will be posted on Ed. Students can use Ed to ask questions.
- **Gradescope:** Some homework assignments will require submissions via Gradescope. Notifications for submissions will be provided on Ed and during lectures.

### PSO Schedule
- PSOs will begin in the second week of the semester. Attendance is optional.
- **Locations:** 
  - Indianapolis: SL 247
  - West Lafayette: HAAS G050
- PSO schedule will be posted at the end of week 1.

### Communication Protocol
- **Course Ed Group Registration:** Sign up using this link: [Ed Signup](https://edstem.org/us/join/Ym5mMG) 

Communication from the Instructor and TAs:
- Class announcements will predominantly be made via Ed.

Communication from You to the Instructor and TAs:
1. Discuss questions with classmates.
2. Use the search feature on Ed before posting questions to find similar inquiries.
3. Post a public question on Ed. Anonymity is an option for asking and answering.
4. Attend office hours for direct interaction with the instructor or TAs.
5. Use private questions on Ed for sensitive issues, but note responses may be less immediate. 

### Important Notes
- Public questions will be prioritized. For personal queries, consider rephrasing them for public discussion.
- This structure is designed to encourage peer engagement and maximize the learning environment.


