CS 34800: Information Systems Syllabus - Spring 2026

### Course Information
- **Course Title:** CS 34800: Information Systems
- **CRNs:** 13399 and 35118
- **Instructional Modality:** Face-to-Face
- **Course Credit Hours:** 3
- **Prerequisites:** Data Structures, Discrete Structures, and programming experience.

### Course Description
This course introduces fundamental concepts of database management, primarily using the relational model. Key topics include:
- Schema design and refinement
- Query languages
- Database application development
- Transactions and concurrency control
- Overview of NoSQL databases (including graph and document stores)
- Overview of OLAP and data warehousing

### Course Comparisons
- **CS34800 vs. CS44800 vs. CS44000**
  - **CS34800:** Focuses on application development scenarios (designing and using a database system in web or mobile applications).
  - **CS44000:** Targets data-science and machine learning scenarios, emphasizing large-scale databases.
  - **CS44800:** Concentrates on database system implementation (database internals).

### Course Learning Outcomes
Students will learn to:
- Design effective database schemas that model real-world information semantics.
- Understand different data models (relational, graph, and document) utilized across database systems.
- Pose complex database queries using SQL, NoSQL languages, and theoretical query languages.
- Gain foundational knowledge in indexing.
- Understand transactions and concurrency control.
- Differentiate between OLTP and OLAP databases.

### Homework and TA Information
- **Lead TA:** Each homework will have a designated lead TA for special requests related to the homework (e.g., regrade requests, submission issues).
- **Project TA:** A project TA will be assigned to each student for project grading. Email your project TA with project-related questions.

#### Head TA Contact Information
- **Name:** Xi Pang
- **Email:** [pang65@purdue.edu](mailto:pang65@purdue.edu)

The head TA handles requests unrelated to specific homework or projects. Common issues include missed quizzes, extension requests, DRC-related accommodations, and makeup exams. Response time is within 24 hours on weekdays; follow up if no response is received.

### Instructor Information
- **Name:** Hisham Benotman
- **Email:** [hbenotma@purdue.edu](mailto:hbenotma@purdue.edu)
- **Office:** DSAI 1119G
- **Office Hours:** By appointment via email. Available after class for urgent questions.

### Teaching Assistants
- Xi Pang (Head TA) - [pang65@purdue.edu](mailto:pang65@purdue.edu)
- Yinqi Sun (HW1 Lead TA) - [sun1226@purdue.edu](mailto:sun1226@purdue.edu)
