================WEBPAGE: 2278=================

CS 34800: Information Systems Syllabus - Spring 2026

### Course Information
- **Course Title:** CS 34800: Information Systems
- **CRNs:** 13399 and 35118
- **Instructional Modality:** Face-to-Face
- **Course Credit Hours:** 3
- **Prerequisites:** Data Structures, Discrete Structures, and programming experience.

### Course Description
This course introduces fundamental concepts of database management, primarily using the relational model. Key topics include:
- Schema design and refinement
- Query languages
- Database application development
- Transactions and concurrency control
- Overview of NoSQL databases (including graph and document stores)
- Overview of OLAP and data warehousing

### Course Comparisons
- **CS34800 vs. CS44800 vs. CS44000**
  - **CS34800:** Focuses on application development scenarios (designing and using a database system in web or mobile applications).
  - **CS44000:** Targets data-science and machine learning scenarios, emphasizing large-scale databases.
  - **CS44800:** Concentrates on database system implementation (database internals).

### Course Learning Outcomes
Students will learn to:
- Design effective database schemas that model real-world information semantics.
- Understand different data models (relational, graph, and document) utilized across database systems.
- Pose complex database queries using SQL, NoSQL languages, and theoretical query languages.
- Gain foundational knowledge in indexing.
- Understand transactions and concurrency control.
- Differentiate between OLTP and OLAP databases.

### Homework and TA Information
- **Lead TA:** Each homework will have a designated lead TA for special requests related to the homework (e.g., regrade requests, submission issues).
- **Project TA:** A project TA will be assigned to each student for project grading. Email your project TA with project-related questions.

#### Head TA Contact Information
- **Name:** Xi Pang
- **Email:** [pang65@purdue.edu](mailto:pang65@purdue.edu)

The head TA handles requests unrelated to specific homework or projects. Common issues include missed quizzes, extension requests, DRC-related accommodations, and makeup exams. Response time is within 24 hours on weekdays; follow up if no response is received.

### Instructor Information
- **Name:** Hisham Benotman
- **Email:** [hbenotma@purdue.edu](mailto:hbenotma@purdue.edu)
- **Office:** DSAI 1119G
- **Office Hours:** By appointment via email. Available after class for urgent questions.

### Teaching Assistants
- Xi Pang (Head TA) - [pang65@purdue.edu](mailto:pang65@purdue.edu)
- Yinqi Sun (HW1 Lead TA) - [sun1226@purdue.edu](mailto:sun1226@purdue.edu)
- Yeasir Rayhan (HW6 Lead TA) - [yrayhan@purdue.edu](mailto:yrayhan@purdue.edu)
- Chenzhe Jin (HW3 Lead TA) - [jin467@purdue.edu](mailto:jin467@purdue.edu)
- Xiao Hu - [hu953@purdue.edu](mailto:hu953@purdue.edu)
- Yunxin Sun (HW2 Lead TA) - [sun1114@purdue.edu](mailto:sun1114@purdue.edu)
- Yibo Wang (HW5 Lead TA) - [wang7342@purdue.edu](mailto:wang7342@purdue.edu)
- Xinzhi Wang (HW4 Lead TA) - [wang6171@purdue.edu](mailto:wang6171@purdue.edu)
- Jerry B. Liu - [jbliu@purdue.edu](mailto:jbliu@purdue.edu)

### TA Office Hours
TA office hours will take place every weekday from 4:00 PM to 6:00 PM (excluding holidays).  
Join TA office hours via this link: [Zoom link](https://purdue-edu.zoom.us/j/98460606670)  
In-person UTA office hours schedule will be posted soon.

### Textbook
- **Recommended:** Database System Concepts, 7th Edition by Avi Silberschatz, Henry F. Korth, and S. Sudarshan.
  - ISBN (Paperback): 978-1260084504
    - [Amazon Link](https://www.amazon.com/dp/126008450X)
  - ISBN (eTextbook, hardcover, or loose-leaf): 978-0078022159
    - [Amazon Link](https://www.amazon.com/dp/0078022158)

The textbook webpage provides additional resources such as slides, answers for practice questions, and an SQL interpreter at [db-book.com](http://www.db-book.com).

### Lectures
- **Meeting Times:** 
  - Indianapolis: **MW 5:30 PM to 6:45 PM**, ET 202.
  - West Lafayette: **TuTh 10:30 AM to 11:45 AM**, BHEE 129.

Lecture slides will be available at least one hour before the lecture. Recordings will be posted on Brightspace unless technical issues occur. They may be available as one or multiple videos. A subset of slides will be posted a few days after lectures. Note that solutions to in-class quizzes are not included in the posted materials. All class content is copyrighted.

### Course Schedule with Activity Due Dates
- **Schedule Information:** The schedule is tentative and subject to change. Due date changes will be highlighted in red.
  - View the schedule here: [Course Schedule](https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1mov5_UbuG9F6wt_QZWm8yGn4kfpkYqokqkzzgk0kwOU/edit?usp=sharing)

### Course Content and Links
- **Brightspace:** Content such as the syllabus, course schedule, lecture slides, and lecture videos will be uploaded to Brightspace. Quizzes and submission for some homework assignments will also be conducted via Brightspace.
- **Ed:** Class announcements and student questions will be posted on Ed. Students can use Ed to ask questions.
- **Gradescope:** Some homework assignments will require submissions via Gradescope. Notifications for submissions will be provided on Ed and during lectures.

### PSO Schedule
- PSOs will begin in the second week of the semester. Attendance is optional.
- **Locations:** 
  - Indianapolis: SL 247
  - West Lafayette: HAAS G050
- PSO schedule will be posted at the end of week 1.

### Communication Protocol
- **Course Ed Group Registration:** Sign up using this link: [Ed Signup](https://edstem.org/us/join/Ym5mMG) 

Communication from the Instructor and TAs:
- Class announcements will predominantly be made via Ed.

Communication from You to the Instructor and TAs:
1. Discuss questions with classmates.
2. Use the search feature on Ed before posting questions to find similar inquiries.
3. Post a public question on Ed. Anonymity is an option for asking and answering.
4. Attend office hours for direct interaction with the instructor or TAs.
5. Use private questions on Ed for sensitive issues, but note responses may be less immediate. 

### Important Notes
- Public questions will be prioritized. For personal queries, consider rephrasing them for public discussion.
- This structure is designed to encourage peer engagement and maximize the learning environment.
================WEBPAGE: 6408=================

Purdue University Course Outline
Department of Computer Science

### CS 44100: Data Science Capstone
**Spring 2026**  
**CRN:** 20433  

**Course Prerequisites:** CS 373  
**Course Brightspace link:** [Purdue Brightspace](https://purdue.brightspace.com/d2l/home/1488420)  

**Instructor**  
**Professor:** Mary Anne Smart  
**E-Mail:** [masmart@purdue.edu](mailto:masmart@purdue.edu)  
**Office Hours:** Tuesday, 2:30pm – 3:30pm in DSAI 1119D or by appointment  

**Graduate Teaching Assistants (GTAs)**  
- **Sai Coumar**  
  **E-Mail:** [scoumar@purdue.edu](mailto:scoumar@purdue.edu)  
- **Shrutheesh Iyer**  
  **E-Mail:** [iyer270@purdue.edu](mailto:iyer270@purdue.edu)  
- **Pinaki Mohanty**  
  **E-Mail:** [pmohanty@purdue.edu](mailto:pmohanty@purdue.edu)  

### Lectures  
Lectures are held on Mondays and Wednesdays from 4:30pm to 5:45pm in Hicks Undergraduate Library G980D. Some lecture time will be available for project meetings and collaboration as the semester progresses. Audio from class meetings will be recorded via BoilerCast. Contact the instructor for access to recordings if necessary.

### Course Description  
This capstone course provides students with the opportunity to integrate their accumulated knowledge and skills to identify and solve realistic data science problems, focusing on practical applications. Projects may be sponsored by corporate partners or academic/non-academic research groups. Students will follow the six phases of the CRISP-DM (Cross-Industry Standard Process for Data Mining) methodology for a team-based project, gaining experience in project planning, report writing, and presentations.

### Learning Objectives  
After successful completion of this course, students will be able to:  
- Identify the business objectives in a complex problem.  
- Develop appropriate data science goals and project plans.  
- Implement appropriate data pre-processing and analysis models.  
- Evaluate models and interpret results with respect to business objectives.  
- Present project outcomes through written reports and digital/oral presentations.  
(Note: "business" refers to the corporate partner or research group sponsoring the capstone projects.)

### Textbooks  
There is no official textbook for this class. The following materials may be useful:  
- **Principles of Data Mining** by David Hand, Heikki Mannila, Padhraic Smyth  
- **A Course in Machine Learning** by Hal Daumé III  
- **Pattern Recognition and Machine Learning** by Christopher M. Bishop  
- **Machine Learning** by Tom Mitchell  
- **Pattern Classification** by Richard O. Duda, Peter E. Hart, David G. Stork  

### Grade Calculation  
**Team Participation (80%)**  
- Business Understanding Report: 10%  
- Data Understanding Report: 10%  
- Data Preparation Report: 15%  
- Modeling Report: 25%  
- Evaluation Report: 20%  

**Individual Participation (20%)**  
- Presentation of Team Project: 15%  
- Participation: 5%  

Note: Presentation scores will reflect the best performance across the semester. Overall team participation scores will be subject to a peer evaluation multiplier. Individual participation can be earned by attending class and completing peer evaluations.

### Grade Divisions  
- A+ [99-100]  
- A [93-99)  
- A- [90-93)  
- B+ [86-90)  
- B [83-86)  
- B- [80-83)  
- C+ [76-80)  
- C [73-76)  
- C- [70-73)  
- D+ [66-70)  
- D [63-66)  
- D- [60-63)  

These thresholds may be adjusted slightly in favor of more generosity.

### Late Policy  
Late submissions incur a 5% penalty per 24-hour period and may be submitted up to three days late. Additional extensions may be granted at the discretion of the instructional staff if arranged in advance. Documentation is required for agreements regarding late submissions. Extensions exceeding one week are only granted for serious and documented emergencies, processed through the Dean of Students’ Office: [Purdue Absences](https://www.purdue.edu/advocacy/students/absences.html).

### Regrade Requests  
Requests for regrades must be made within seven days of receiving the grade, specifying what needs reassessment and why. Generic requests may be dismissed. No regrading will occur after this period.

### Channels of Communication  
Questions about lecture content and reports should be posted in Ed discussion. For serious or confidential topics, email the instructor at [masmart@purdue.edu](mailto:masmart@purdue.edu) to schedule an appointment. Announcements will be made via Brightspace, and students are expected to monitor both Brightspace and email for class-related information. Respectful online interaction is encouraged, in line with the department's netiquette policy: [Department Netiquette Policy](https://www.cs.purdue.edu/resources/instructional/netiquette.html).

### Academic Integrity  
Purdue University prohibits dishonesty in any university activity, including cheating and plagiarism. Review the departmental academic integrity policy here: [Academic Integrity Policy](https://www.cs.purdue.edu/resources/academic_integrity.html). It is encouraged to discuss and help each other with basic concepts, but submitted work must reflect individual efforts.

### Artificial Intelligence Policy  
The use of AI must be clearly disclosed in reports and presentations. Failure to disclose AI use constitutes academic dishonesty.

### Nondiscrimination Statement  
Purdue University is committed to valuing the inherent worth of every person and fostering respect and understanding. More information is available on the course Brightspace under University Policies.

### Accessibility  
Purdue University is committed to accessibility for all students. Students experiencing barriers due to a disability should contact the Disability Resource Center at 765-494-1247 or [drc@purdue.edu](mailto:drc@purdue.edu) for accommodations.

### Mental Health Statement  
- **Therapy Assistance Online (TAO)** is a free resource for all Purdue students: [TAO Resource](https://www.purdue.edu/caps/students/resources/self-help/digital/tao.php).  
- If you need support or information about personal issues, please reach out to the appropriate resources.  

### Contact Information
For additional details, please refer to your Brightspace course materials or contact relevant faculty members.
