 High-Performance Computing Overview
High-Performance Computing (HPC) at Purdue University involves the design, management, and support of advanced computing resources. It is essential for researchers and academics who require significant computational power for their projects.


__________________________________________________
 How to Request High-Performance Computing Services
To request services related to High-Performance Computing, users should submit their requests through the "Purdue IT Request" button available on the portal. This process allows for the efficient handling of inquiries and service requests.


__________________________________________________
 Reporting Issues
If users encounter problems with any HPC tools or processes, they can report these issues to notify the support staff. This ensures that any disruptions in service are addressed promptly.


__________________________________________________
 Related Articles
One related article addresses common login issues with Scholar RStudio and JupyterHub, providing guidance for users who may experience difficulties accessing these platforms.


__________________________________________________
 Service Offerings
1. **RCAC Service Request**: This form is specifically for submitting service requests and questions directly to the RCAC (Research Computing and Analytics Center) support team.
2. **High-Performance Computing Request**: A dedicated request form for HPC-related inquiries.


__________________________________________________

__________________________________________________
 Context
This chunk serves as a comprehensive guide to the High-Performance Computing services at Purdue, outlining the necessary steps for users to engage with the resources and support available. It is crucial for those involved in research that requires high computational capabilities.
__________________________________________________

WEBPAGE: www.math.purdue.edu_resources_computing_index.html

 Mathematics Computing Overview
The Department of Mathematics at Purdue University promotes the use of computers for various purposes including research, teaching, outreach, and administration. This section outlines the computing resources available to faculty and graduate students, emphasizing the importance of computer literacy, particularly with Linux systems and computer clusters.


__________________________________________________
 Accessing Resources
**Office Computers:** Faculty can request either Windows or Linux machines for their offices. If the desired operating system is unavailable, requests can be sent to sciencehelp@purdue.edu. Student offices are equipped with Linux machines. Office computers are suitable for email, mathematical text processing (TeX, LaTeX), symbolic computation (Maple, Matlab, etc.), and other computational tasks.

**Departmental Computers:** The computers named banach and hardy provide access to more powerful computational servers. Users can log in via Remote Desktop Access or through SSH commands. Off-campus access requires a VPN connection. Note that password-only logins are disabled on these machines; Duo Mobile or SSH key pairs are required for authentication.


__________________________________________________
 Computational Resources
For small computational jobs, users can utilize applications like Maple, Macaulay2, R, SageMath, and Mathematica on banach or hardy. For larger jobs, users can connect to the departmental computer maclaurin. For even greater computational needs, access to departmental CPU and GPU servers is available.


__________________________________________________
 RCAC Nodes Access
Starting Spring 2024, the department has access to Purdue’s GPU cluster (gilbreth) and CPU cluster (negishi) at the Rosen Center for Advanced Computing (RCAC). New users must request an account specifying “Mathematics Department” as their research group. After approval, users can access these clusters through designated URLs and utilize interactive applications like MATLAB and Jupyter Notebook.


__________________________________________________
 GoRemote/Citrix Applications
Purdue IT offers access to various applications campus-wide through GoRemote/Citrix, including Git, Maple, Matlab, and RStudio. Users can log in with their Purdue Career username and password, with an option for a "Light version" that does not require Citrix installation.


__________________________________________________
 Additional Hardware and Support
The Mathematics department maintains computer labs (MATH 635 and MATH 737) with public printers and desktop computers. Laptops and projectors are available for checkout through the main office. Purdue IT provides broader computing services, including instructional and research support, and can be contacted for assistance.


__________________________________________________
Important Resources
- Eduroam service
- Helpdesk Request Form
- Remote access to department services
- Personal web page setup for faculty and graduate students
__________________________________________________

================WEBPAGE: 3161=================

WEBPAGE: www.cs.purdue.edu_resources_undergrad-accounts.html

 Department of Computer Science Overview
The Department of Computer Science at Purdue University provides a range of resources and facilities for undergraduate students. This includes access to instructional labs, remote access to UNIX/Linux workstations, and various computing resources. The department emphasizes responsible use of these facilities and has established policies to guide students in their usage.


__________________________________________________
Lab Access and Disk Space Policy
- **Lab Access**: CS instructional labs are available during open hours. Remote access to UNIX/Linux workstations via SSH is permitted at all times.
- **Disk Space**: As of Fall 2012, undergraduate accounts have a quota of 1GB for career and instructional accounts.
__________________________________________________
Usage Policies for Computing Facilities
- **Games and Non-Academic Use**: Students are encouraged to use resources reasonably. If gaming or non-academic activities disrupt others, action will be taken, including account suspension.
- **Food and Drink**: Consumption of food and drinks in labs is discouraged to prevent spills and maintain cleanliness.
- **Account Use**: Sharing accounts is strictly prohibited. Violations can lead to account suspension.
__________________________________________________
Printing and Backup Policies
- **Printing**: Students are urged to minimize printing to reduce costs and environmental impact. They should utilize online previewers and print documents duplexed and "two up" when possible.
- **Backups and Restores**: User files are backed up regularly. If files are deleted, students can request restoration through the trouble web form or by contacting IT staff.
__________________________________________________
Reporting Problems and Violations
- **Reporting Issues**: Students should report problems using the trouble web form available on the Help page.
- **Policy Violations**: Violations of the policies may result in account freezing for a specified duration.
__________________________________________________
Contact Information
- **IT Staff Contact**: For assistance, students can reach out to the IT staff.
- **Department Location**: Department of Computer Science, 305 N. University Street, West Lafayette, IN 47907.
- **Phone**: 765-494-6010.
__________________________________________________
 Context
This document serves as a comprehensive guide to the resources, policies, and procedures within the Department of Computer Science at Purdue University. It is designed to help undergraduate students navigate the computing facilities, understand their responsibilities, and utilize the available support effectively. Each section is tailored to address specific aspects of student life within the department, ensuring a well-rounded understanding of the environment they are part of.
__________________________________________________

